1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,854
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,854 --> 00:00:08,216
This is chapter twenty-two: immutability and defensive copying.

3
00:00:08,666 --> 00:00:12,451
Immutability removes whole categories of bugs at once.

4
00:00:12,451 --> 00:00:19,319
Nothing can corrupt the object, no thread can see it half-updated, and its invariants can't drift.

5
00:00:19,319 --> 00:00:21,421
The cost is mostly discipline.

6
00:00:21,421 --> 00:00:23,594
And that's where it goes wrong.

7
00:00:23,594 --> 00:00:28,079
Most supposedly immutable classes in real codebases have a hole.

8
00:00:28,979 --> 00:00:31,061
Here's an order that looks safe.

9
00:00:31,061 --> 00:00:33,599
The class is final, the field is final.

10
00:00:33,599 --> 00:00:39,454
But the constructor stores the caller's list, and the accessor hands it straight back out.

11
00:00:39,904 --> 00:00:44,150
So there are three ways to change it without touching the class.

12
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:47,135
The caller still holds the list it passed in.

13
00:00:47,135 --> 00:00:49,722
The accessor exposes the internal list.

14
00:00:49,722 --> 00:00:52,508
And the line items themselves are mutable.

15
00:00:52,508 --> 00:00:55,294
final stops you reassigning the reference.

16
00:00:55,294 --> 00:00:58,677
It does nothing about what the reference points to.

17
00:00:59,577 --> 00:01:02,982
There are four different things people call immutable.

18
00:01:02,982 --> 00:01:08,025
A final field: you can still modify the list through it, and so can anyone else.

19
00:01:08,475 --> 00:01:14,769
An unmodifiable view: you can't modify it, but the owner can, and you'll see those changes.

20
00:01:15,219 --> 00:01:21,320
List dot copyOf: a real snapshot, but if the elements are mutable, they can still change.

21
00:01:21,770 --> 00:01:27,208
Only a copied list of immutable elements, like records, is actually immutable.

22
00:01:27,208 --> 00:01:30,346
The rest are useful, but each one has a hole.

23
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:32,459
Here's the recipe.

24
00:01:32,459 --> 00:01:37,646
Make the class final, or use a record, so a subclass can't add mutable state.

25
00:01:38,096 --> 00:01:40,635
Private final fields, and no setters.

26
00:01:41,085 --> 00:01:47,101
Copy every mutable input inside the constructor, so the caller can't keep a handle on your state.

27
00:01:47,551 --> 00:01:52,351
Store immutable types, so your accessors don't need to copy on the way out.

28
00:01:52,801 --> 00:01:56,342
And make sure the components are immutable all the way down.

29
00:01:57,242 --> 00:01:58,917
Here's the order fixed.

30
00:01:58,917 --> 00:01:59,937
It's a record.

31
00:01:59,937 --> 00:02:07,511
The compact constructor validates, then copies the lines with List dot copyOf, which also rejects nulls.

32
00:02:07,511 --> 00:02:10,789
And OrderLine is a record of immutable parts.

33
00:02:11,239 --> 00:02:13,964
All three mutations are now impossible.

34
00:02:13,964 --> 00:02:19,695
And no copy is needed in the accessor, because what's stored is already immutable.

35
00:02:19,695 --> 00:02:25,425
Copy on the way in, store an immutable type, and the way out takes care of itself.

36
00:02:25,875 --> 00:02:32,658
Better still, List dot copyOf doesn't copy if its argument is already an immutable JDK list.

37
00:02:32,658 --> 00:02:34,059
It just returns it.

38
00:02:34,059 --> 00:02:41,136
So in a codebase that uses records and List dot of throughout, defensive copying is nearly free.

39
00:02:41,136 --> 00:02:46,739
It does copy an unmodifiable view, correctly, since a view can still change.

40
00:02:47,639 --> 00:02:49,745
Records don't help with arrays.

41
00:02:49,745 --> 00:02:56,875
A byte array component can be changed by the caller who passed it in, and by anyone calling the accessor.

42
00:02:56,875 --> 00:03:08,759
So you'd have to clone on the way in, clone on the way out by overriding the accessor, and override equals and hashCode, because the generated ones compare arrays by identity.

43
00:03:09,209 --> 00:03:14,264
Records are only shallowly immutable, and arrays are never immutable.

44
00:03:14,264 --> 00:03:19,172
Usually the better answer is a list, or a dedicated immutable type.

45
00:03:20,072 --> 00:03:23,692
You change an immutable object by making a new one.

46
00:03:23,692 --> 00:03:29,796
A wither, like withQuantity, returns a new CartLine and leaves the original untouched.

47
00:03:29,796 --> 00:03:35,475
And since the compact constructor runs every time, validation can't be bypassed.

48
00:03:35,925 --> 00:03:42,085
When there are many optional parts, positional construction gets unreadable, so use a builder.

49
00:03:42,085 --> 00:03:44,904
The builder is mutable and single-threaded.

50
00:03:44,904 --> 00:03:47,722
The product is immutable and safe to share.

51
00:03:47,722 --> 00:03:54,079
The record's constructor still validates and copies, so the builder can't produce a leaky object.

52
00:03:54,979 --> 00:03:56,546
Here's the big payoff.

53
00:03:56,546 --> 00:04:06,874
Under the Java Memory Model, an object with all-final fields, properly constructed, can be shared between threads with no synchronisation at all.

54
00:04:06,874 --> 00:04:08,441
No locks, no volatile.

55
00:04:08,891 --> 00:04:09,857
Two conditions.

56
00:04:09,857 --> 00:04:13,593
The reference to this must not escape during construction.

57
00:04:13,593 --> 00:04:22,032
And the guarantee covers the state as of the end of the constructor, which is exactly why the defensive copy must happen inside it.

58
00:04:22,482 --> 00:04:25,710
And immutable isn't necessarily more expensive.

59
00:04:25,710 --> 00:04:31,821
List dot of with two elements is a single small object with two fields, no backing array.

60
00:04:31,821 --> 00:04:34,087
That's smaller than an ArrayList.

61
00:04:34,987 --> 00:04:36,128
What does it cost?

62
00:04:36,128 --> 00:04:39,169
Copying an already-immutable list costs nothing.

63
00:04:39,619 --> 00:04:42,477
A wither is one small, short-lived allocation.

64
00:04:42,927 --> 00:04:48,603
The real cost is appending to a huge immutable collection, which copies everything.

65
00:04:48,603 --> 00:04:51,476
For a million elements that's prohibitive.

66
00:04:51,476 --> 00:04:55,647
Most domain objects have tens of items, so it doesn't matter.

67
00:04:55,647 --> 00:05:02,554
For large, frequently-updated collections, use a mutable builder, or a persistent collection library.

68
00:05:03,004 --> 00:05:05,397
And remember the inheritance hole.

69
00:05:05,397 --> 00:05:11,660
If the class isn't final, a subclass can override an accessor and return changing values.

70
00:05:11,660 --> 00:05:16,657
That's why records, which are implicitly final, are the safest default.

71
00:05:17,557 --> 00:05:18,819
Production rules.

72
00:05:18,819 --> 00:05:23,865
Default to records, with List dot copyOf in the compact constructor.

73
00:05:23,865 --> 00:05:26,463
Copy inputs inside the constructor.

74
00:05:26,463 --> 00:05:28,096
Store immutable types.

75
00:05:28,096 --> 00:05:32,029
Use withers for change and builders for many options.

76
00:05:32,029 --> 00:05:34,404
Replace Date with java dot time.

77
00:05:34,404 --> 00:05:36,779
And return snapshots, not views.

78
00:05:37,229 --> 00:05:39,749
Never assume final means immutable.

79
00:05:39,749 --> 00:05:46,518
Never return a mutable internal collection, or store a caller's collection without copying it.

80
00:05:46,518 --> 00:05:50,478
Never put arrays in a record without copying both ways.

81
00:05:50,478 --> 00:05:53,575
Never treat unmodifiableList as a snapshot.

82
00:05:53,575 --> 00:05:56,599
And never let this escape the constructor.

83
00:05:57,499 --> 00:05:58,139
To recap.

84
00:05:58,139 --> 00:06:01,122
final fixes the reference, not the object.

85
00:06:01,122 --> 00:06:07,017
Copy on the way in and store immutable types, and the way out takes care of itself.

86
00:06:07,017 --> 00:06:11,279
List dot copyOf is free when its input is already immutable.

87
00:06:11,279 --> 00:06:15,825
And properly constructed immutable objects need no locks at all.

88
00:06:16,275 --> 00:06:20,566
Next is chapter twenty-three: equals, hashCode and comparators.

89
00:06:20,566 --> 00:06:21,928
Thanks for watching.
